TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup structural roles",
    "organizer role",
    "shaper role",
    "terminal role",
    "lineup coverage",
    "within-team variation of roles",
    "separability of roles from talent/counts",
    "talent control"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define and measure 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles using play-by-play and derived play-type data?",
    "How to aggregate individual player roles into 'lineup coverage' (e.g., >=1 of each role)?",
    "What statistical methods can demonstrate within-team variation in lineup coverage?",
    "What statistical methods can establish the separability of structural roles from individual player talent metrics or raw statistical counts?",
    "How to operationalize and measure 'talent control' in the context of structural roles and analyze its 'behavior'?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available from prior project research."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available, so nothing is established or left open by it."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question posits three distinct claims: (1) within-team variation of lineup coverage, (2) separability of roles from talent/counts, and (3) 'talent control behaves'.",
    "The 'derived play-type possession records (initiator, play type, points)' are crucial for operationalizing structural roles. For example, 'organizer' might be defined by high initiator rates in certain play types, 'terminal' by high scoring rates as the possession finisher, and 'shaper' by actions that create advantages for others (e.g., screens, off-ball movement leading to assists) even if not initiating or finishing.",
    "The phrase 'talent control behaves' is open to interpretation. It likely refers to how teams strategically manage the presence of players fulfilling these structural roles across different lineups or game situations (e.g., do they maintain minimum coverage, or does it vary predictably with game state or opponent?). This will require a specific, testable hypothesis to be formulated.",
    "Separability from 'talent/counts' implies that the identified roles are not simply redundant with common player statistics (e.g., points, assists, usage rate) but represent a distinct structural contribution."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition and measurement of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles from the available play-type data are not specified and will be a primary challenge.",
    "The precise meaning of 'talent control behaves' is ambiguous and requires clear operationalization before testing.",
    "The granularity and reliability of the 'derived play-type possession records' are unknown, which directly impacts the feasibility of role definition."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available to inform or change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.6,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The core challenge is reliably defining 'organizer,' 'shaper,' and 'terminal' roles from the available play-by-play and derived play-type data. Without clear, validated definitions, the entire premise of the research is untestable. This requires a significant upfront methodology development effort."
      },
      {
        "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
        "text": "The structural roles are derived labels. The process of defining these labels must be carefully designed to avoid circularity, especially when attempting to demonstrate 'separability from talent/counts.' If the role definitions implicitly incorporate talent metrics, proving separability becomes trivial or impossible."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work from this project addressing this specific research question was retrieved."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Defining 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles consistently and accurately from play-by-play data, particularly the nuanced 'shaper' role, is difficult and may lack external validation. Different definitions could lead to different conclusions."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Demonstrating 'separability from talent/counts' is challenging. Player talent (e.g., scoring efficiency, passing ability) is inherently linked to who can effectively perform these structural roles. Confounding variables related to player skill, coaching schemes, and game situation must be carefully controlled when attempting to show separability."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "The structural roles themselves are derived labels. The method used to assign these roles could inadvertently embed aspects of player talent or raw counts, making the claim of 'separability' problematic or tautological. Care must be taken to define roles based on structural actions rather than outcomes or individual statistics."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Certain lineup configurations (e.g., specific combinations of roles) or players fulfilling very niche roles might have limited minutes/possessions, leading to sparse data for some analyses, particularly for within-team variation or specific 'talent control' patterns."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Errors in the initial classification of play types, initiators, or other components within the derived play-type possession records could propagate and lead to misclassification of player roles, affecting the validity of subsequent analyses."
    },
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "If nearly all lineups inherently have at least one player who could be categorized into each role, demonstrating 'variation' might become trivial or lack meaningful insight. The baseline frequency of these roles needs to be considered."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.2,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.4,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The research aims to estimate: (1) the within-team variance of lineup coverage (presence of >=1 organizer, shaper, terminal) across different lineups; (2) the unique contribution of structural roles to lineup performance after controlling for individual player talent/counts; (3) patterns and determinants of how teams deploy players in these structural roles ('talent control').",
    "unit": "The primary unit of analysis will be the lineup-possession. Lineups are defined by the five players on the court. Player roles will be aggregated to the lineup level for each possession.",
    "sample": "All available NBA regular season and playoff possessions from 2022-23 to 2025-26 (partial), totaling 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted after initial definitions of roles and lineup coverage are established. Given 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons, there is likely sufficient power to detect meaningful effects, provided role definitions are robust and not overly sparse. Initial estimates of within-team variance and correlations will guide power calculations.",
    "controls": "When assessing separability from talent/counts, analyses will control for individual player talent metrics (e.g., usage rate, points per possession, assist rate, rebound rate, efficiency metrics) within a lineup. Game context variables (e.g., score differential, quarter, opponent strength) will also be controlled. For 'talent control' analysis, team-level talent distribution and coaching preferences will be considered.",
    "falsification": "The hypothesis of within-team variation would be rejected if lineup coverage remains largely constant within teams, or if observed variation is not statistically significant. The hypothesis of separability would be rejected if structural roles are found to be fully explained by or redundant with individual player talent/counts. The 'talent control behaves' hypothesis would be rejected if no discernible or predictable patterns of talent deployment in structural roles are found.",
    "multiplicity": "Given three distinct sub-hypotheses, appropriate adjustments for multiple comparisons (e.g., Bonferroni correction, False Discovery Rate control) will be applied if conducting multiple statistical tests on related outcomes. Alternatively, a hierarchical modeling approach will be considered.",
    "decision_rule": "Statistical significance will be assessed at p < 0.05. Effect sizes will also be reported to assess practical significance. For 'talent control behaves,' qualitative assessment of patterns will precede quantitative modeling.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular season and playoffs, using per-game files from the NBA stats API and derived play-type possession records in the graph database, as available on 2026-07-13."
  }
}

